eos/messages.py, eos/params.py
  The SysEx frame layout, command set, and parameter ID tables are transcribed
  from the manufacturer's own protocol specification:
    "Remote Preset Editing via MIDI SysEx", Draft #30, EOS 4.00
    Brian Clark, E-mu Systems, 17 February 1999
  That document is NOT redistributed with this project.

  Most of what is taken from it is wire-protocol fact used as data — command
  bytes, parameter ids, value ranges, and the specification's own names for
  parameters and enumerated values.

  One exception, stated plainly: the specification also contains a small
  amount of C source for the display conversions that turn a raw parameter
  value into the number the front panel shows, and the following functions in
  eos/params.py follow it directly rather than being independently derived:

    fil_freq()          a port of the spec's fil_freq(input, maxfreq, mul)
    filter_table_1/2/3  its (maxfreq, mul) arguments per Filter Tables 1-3
    cnv_morph_freq()    per the spec's Filter Table 5

  These are a few lines of arithmetic each, reproduced for interoperability —
  to display the same value the device itself displays — and E-mu / Creative
  Technology Ltd. retain whatever rights subsist in the original. If that
  is unacceptable to the rights holder, these functions can be removed or
  reimplemented from observed input/output behaviour without affecting any
  other part of this project.

  Everything else here is original work. Released under GPL-2.0-or-later.

probes/panel_capture.py, docs/captures/, RESOLUTION_NOTES §3/§26-§28
  (panel/remote protocol -- F0 18 7F ... , NOT the editor protocol above)

  E-mu DID document this protocol, in "Remote Control of the Emulator-IV
  Series via MIDI/SMDI" (the Peptalk document, 1996) -- a document this
  project did not have and could not find while the work was done, and which
  came to light only afterwards. The RE recorded here was therefore genuinely
  independent, and everything derived here agrees with E-mu's document: all
  six message opcodes (10h, 40h, 43h, 50h, 51h, 52h) and all 37 button IDs
  captured, including six the source had marked "inferred" because they were
  never pressed individually. No disagreement in either direction on anything
  this project captured. An earlier version of this file asserted that no
  such documentation existed. It should have said no documentation had been
  found -- absence of evidence stated as evidence of absence.

  The session-open handshake and the
  fact that a key press emits a down/up pair were published in 2016, before
  this project existed, by an independent third party who observed the
  traffic between Ray Bellis's e-remote and an E4 Ultra:
    "Talking SysEx with E-MU Ultra", midimachines, 30 April 2016
    https://midimachines.wordpress.com/2016/04/30/arduino-midi-and-sampler-ultra-series/

  What this project takes from that page is prior public knowledge about a
  manufacturer's wire protocol -- opcodes 10h (open) and the 7F 11 00 08
  reply -- which we then re-derived independently against an E4XT Ultra on
  firmware 4.70 and found to be framed differently there (§28: the header is
  <devID> 7Ah, not the 00 00 that page records). The opcodes were right; the
  frame layout in the published fragments does not match this firmware.

  That published page is the ONLY external input to this protocol's RE, and
  what was taken from it is the opcodes above -- nothing else. Ray Bellis's
  e-remote (https://emu.tools) was NOT used: not as a traffic source, not as
  a reference, and no part of it was decompiled, disassembled, read or copied.
  An earlier draft of this file said e-remote had been used for one handshake
  capture. That was wrong -- it described a method proposed before hardware
  access and deliberately not taken -- and it is corrected here.

  Every key code, the down/up pairing, the data dial's signed encoding and
  the frame layout were derived from this project's own captures of the
  device echoing PHYSICAL PRESSES on its own front panel, one control at a
  time, with the operator naming each press. Those captures are in
  docs/captures/ and contain device-to-host frames only -- no host-to-device
  traffic of any kind, from e-remote or anything else.

  eosed does now mirror the LCD and send keypresses (front-panel mode, `k`),
  which an earlier scope decision had ruled out. That decision was reversed
  on 2026-08-16 and the implementation is built entirely on the captures
  described above.

  The display-frame decoding in §26 -- 240x64 monochrome, standard MIDI 7->8
  packing, row-major at 30 bytes per row -- is not published on that page or
  anywhere else found, and was derived here from this project's own captures.
  Original work.

eos/bridge.py
  The throttled-output queue (ThrottledOut), the MultiIn merged-input facade,
  the ALSA-client leak fix, and the MIDI port-enumeration helpers are ported
  from the sibling k2kremote project (k2kremote/midi_bridge.py), which itself
  ports them from:
    mpc2emu (tests/re_banks/krz_sysex_live.py)
    Copyright (C) 2025-2026  mpc2emu contributors
    License: GNU General Public License v2.0 or later (GPL-2.0-or-later)
  Both k2kremote and mpc2emu are sibling projects by the same author, also
  released under GPL-2.0-or-later. The rest of this file (EosBridge, the
  config.toml read/write helpers) is original work.

eos/params.py (LFO rate display)
  cnv_lfo_rate()'s byte->Hz coefficients are NOT from the E-mu specification --
  its LFO rate display table could not be transcribed unambiguously (the source
  wraps across a PDF page boundary). They are the sibling mpc2emu project's
  empirical calibration, measured from the E4XT's own rate menu:
    mpc2emu models/common.py, lfo_rate_byte_to_hz()
    Copyright (C) 2025-2026  mpc2emu contributors
    License: GNU General Public License v2.0 or later (GPL-2.0-or-later)

eosed/app.py
  Mostly original work, except: the key-hint legend's fold-to-terminal-width
  algorithm (wrap_blocks) is ported from the sibling k2kremote project
  (k2kremote/app.py's wrap_blocks), same author, released under
  GPL-2.0-or-later.

All other files (eos/__init__.py, eosed/__init__.py, eosed/cli.py,
eosed/demo.py, tests/, tools/)
  Original work. Released under GPL-2.0-or-later.

